package leaderboard

import (
	svcrank "github.com/tjbdwanghaibo/roost-core/service/rank"
)

// This file is the leaderboard module's project-owned half: which board,
// which season, what a legal score is and how long a page is. The module
// (roost module add leaderboard) ships a server-wide total-score board here;
// the demo replaces this one file with its own board and leaves the module's
// logic (leaderboard.go: Submit / SubmitSeason, the two read protocols) as
// installed. leaderboard_board_test.go pins the numbers below.
//
// The demo's board counts cleared dungeon runs, per season. The rank service
// ranks numbers and has no opinion about what they count; the decision worth
// reading is what a point is.
//
// A point is a player's count of paid clears this season, as the claim
// transaction reported it (dungeon.ClaimResult.Clears). The FinishDungeon
// endpoint submits it with leaderboard.SubmitSeason: a SNAPSHOT of a count
// the Player owns (PlayerDao.DungeonClears), brought up with rank UpdateMax
// and no request id — not a "+1". The store fills the tiebreak with the
// submit time and UpdateMax keeps the earlier one for an equal value, so
// players on the same number of clears are ordered by who got there first.
//
// Why not "+1 deduplicated on the run id", which this board used first: the
// rank service remembers only the last few request ids per owner
// (service/rank maxAppliedRequests = 8). That is a redelivery guard, not a
// ledger, and the FinishDungeon endpoint has to submit on every replay so a
// point lost to a crash can be filled. A run replayed inside its claim window
// after eight later clears had fallen out of the ring and counted again (nine
// clears scored ten). Making the ring longer only moves the cliff; tying it to
// the claim window would be a third bounded ledger whose forgetting has to be
// argued separately from the reward's. A count that moves inside the reward
// transaction needs neither: the reward ledger and its window are the only
// admission, the count is never pruned, and UpdateMax makes duplicates and
// out-of-order snapshots harmless.

// BoardID is the clears board's name in the rank service.
const BoardID = "clears"

// CurrentSeason is the season clears are counted for and the board is read
// in. One season for now: a season boundary is an operator action (the
// service deliberately keeps Reset off the bus) and the demo has no calendar,
// so moving it means changing this number and releasing. The count behind
// the board is kept per season on the Player (PlayerDao.DungeonClears), so a
// new season starts every player at zero on a new, empty board instead of
// carrying the old season's count over (maintainer decision 2026-10-18).
//
// It is a function because the endpoint must use ONE value for both halves
// of a clear: it reads the season once, the claim transaction counts the
// clear for it, and SubmitSeason puts that count on that season's board.
func CurrentSeason() int64 {
	return 1
}

// Board is the clears board of one season: global scope, one board for every
// server. The read protocols and the submit both go through it; a client
// never names a board.
func Board(season int64) svcrank.Board {
	return svcrank.Board{ID: BoardID, Scope: svcrank.ScopeGlobal, Season: season}
}

// The legal range of a clear count, both ends included. A player with no
// paid clear this season is not on the board (the endpoint submits only a
// positive count). The upper end is far above what a season of dungeon runs
// can reach; it only stops a wrong number, because the board never goes down
// and a wrong large value could not be corrected by later submits.
const (
	MinScore int64 = 1
	MaxScore int64 = 1_000_000_000
)

// The page the read protocol serves: at most ten rows — a request for fewer
// is honoured, anything else gets ten. The rank service caps a page; "the whole board" is not an option the API offers,
// which is the right default for something that grows with the player base.
const (
	DefaultTop = 10
	MaxTop     = 10
)
